    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d537">

                <pb facs="0628" n="526" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg526"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 Nelson, Bert N./167: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in China (Johnson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-04-06T17:00:00-05:00">April 6, 1933—5 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>113. With further reference to the capture and reported murder of Reverend Bert
                        Nelson,<anchor n="43" xml:id="d537-f1"/> Department desires to know whether,
                    in <pb facs="0629" n="527" xml:id="pg527"/> the considered opinions of the
                    Legation and the Consul General at Hankow, every possible practicable effort has
                    been made by Chinese officials to arrive at a solution of this case or whether,
                    as in the Baker case,<anchor n="44" xml:id="d537-f2"/> more assiduous
                    representations to the Chinese Government might lead to more satisfactory
                    results than have hitherto been attained.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d537-f1" n="43" xml:id="d537-f1-content">
                <p>For previous correspondence concerning the kidnaping of Rev. Bert N. Nelson on
                    October 5, 1930, see <ref target="frus1930v02#pg_197"><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Foreign Relations</hi>, 1930, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>,
                        pp. 197</ref>–<ref target="frus1930v02#pg_223">223</ref>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">passim</hi>; <ref target="frus1931v03#pg_934"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, 1931, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >iii</hi>, pp. 934</ref>–<ref target="frus1931v03#pg_977">977</ref>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">passim;</hi> and <ref target="frus1932v04#pg_470"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, 1932, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >iv</hi>, pp. 470</ref>–<ref target="frus1932v04#pg_561">561</ref>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">passim.</hi></p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d537-f2" n="44" xml:id="d537-f2-content">
                <p>Capt. Charles Baker was kidnaped January 16, 1932, and released May 31. For
                    correspondence concerning this case, see <ref target="frus1932v04#pg_465"><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1932, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, pp. 465</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus1932v04#pg_530">530</ref>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">passim.</hi></p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
